data.content:
## What Breaks in .NET 11

In EF Core 11, the **sync-over-async opt-in for the Cosmos DB provider is removed entirely**. The `CosmosOptionsExtension` no longer recognizes the flag you used in .NET 10.

All four methods will throw `InvalidOperationException` at runtime because `ToList()`, `First()`, `Count()`, and `SaveChanges()` are synchronous APIs, and the Cosmos provider now **only** supports async execution.

## The Fix

Convert to async equivalents:

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key changes:**
- `ToList()` → `ToListAsync()` + `await`
- `First()` → `FirstAsync()` + `await`
- `Count()` → `CountAsync()` + `await`
- `SaveChanges()` → `SaveChangesAsync()` + `await`
- All return types wrapped in `Task<T>`

This will ripple up the call stack — callers must also become `async`. You can also remove any `.EnableSyncOverAsync()` configuration from your `DbContext` setup since it's no longer recognized.
